  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In 2023, the scholarly article titled \"Optimization of Ace2N based voltage indicators\" has garnered initial recognition with one citation. This work, which explores innovative voltage indicators, has been made publicly available on Figshare under a green open access model.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article \"Optimization of Ace2N based voltage indicators,\" authored by a team of researchers including Eric R. Schreiter and Ahmed Abdelfattah, presents advancements in voltage indicators relevant to multiple fields, including neuroscience and electronic engineering. The research embodies critical concepts such as subthreshold conduction and sensitivity in control systems, aimed at enhancing the sensitivity and functionality of fluorescent voltage indicators used in biological studies.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Although the dataset has only seen two views since its publication, its academic impact is beginning to take shape, evidenced by its citation in a 2023 preprint concerning the imaging of neuronal voltage in larval zebrafish. This citation indicates that the work is being recognized within the broader research community and is positioned to influence future studies in related disciplines.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The collaborative effort from prominent institutions, notably Graz University of Technology and Howard Hughes Medical Institute, showcases the interdisciplinary approach to tackling challenges in voltage measurement technology. Researchers and practitioners in fields like computer science and electronic engineering may find this work particularly valuable, as it bridges experimental techniques with theoretical frameworks.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">As the research landscape evolves, continued monitoring of usage and citation trends for this dataset will be essential to fully assess its impact. Future studies leveraging these optimized voltage indicators may pave the way for innovative applications in bioimaging and neuroengineering, emphasizing the importance of accessible research outputs in advancing scientific knowledge.</p>",
